    <h2>The Real Job Is Protecting the Foundation</h2>

    <p>Morgan County sits on clay that swells when it's wet and shrinks when it's dry, and nothing feeds that cycle like roof water dumped at the base of the wall. A 2,000-square-foot roof sheds an enormous volume in a single Alabama downpour, and if that water pours off the eave or out of a downspout right at the foundation, it drives the clay through its worst swelling exactly where the house bears its weight. That's the mechanism behind a lot of the cracked slabs, heaved patios, and wet crawlspaces homeowners here blame on bad luck. Gutters that catch the water and carry it away are, functionally, foundation insurance — and that framing changes how you judge the whole system.</p>

    <h2>The Detail Almost Everyone Gets Wrong</h2>

    <p>Here is the practitioner truth most homeowners never hear: the downspout discharge matters more than the gutter. You can hang perfect seamless gutters and still wreck the foundation if the downspouts empty at the base of the wall, because you've just concentrated the whole roof's water into a few spots right against the clay. The water has to be carried 4 to 6 feet out — with extensions or a buried drain line to daylight — before it's released. What goes wrong on the cheap installs: the crew hangs the gutter, elbows the downspout straight down to the dirt, and calls it done, which can actually be worse than no gutter because it channels the water to a few concentrated points instead of letting it spread. A gutter installer who talks about where the water goes, not just how the gutter looks, is the one who understands the job here.</p>

    <h2>Two Housing Types, Two Gutter Problems</h2>

    <p>Hartselle's older homes near downtown have the fascia problem: decades-old gutters that have overflowed through many storm seasons, quietly rotting the wood board they hang on, so a real job often means replacing fascia before rehanging anything. The newer subdivisions have the capacity problem: builder-standard 5-inch gutters and 2x3 downspouts that can't move what a Morgan County thunderstorm drops, so they overflow at the worst moment and send the water — again — toward the foundation. Stepping up to 6-inch gutters with 3x4 downspouts is the common fix on both. Gutter work also travels with the storm trades: the same spring hail that bends gutters damages roofs, so it's smart to have a <a href="/locations/hartselle/roofer/">roofer</a> flash the new gutters into the drip edge in the same trip, and the black oxidation streaking down an old gutter face comes off with a <a href="/locations/hartselle/pressure-washing/">pressure washing</a> soft-wash rather than a scrub. The honest admission on guards: they cut cleanings way down but no guard under a wooded lot is truly maintenance-free — plan on a check once a year.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Gutters Cost in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, installed:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>5-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $7 to $10 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>6-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $10 to $14 per linear foot, the right call on problem clay or an overflowing home.</li>
      <li><strong>Downspout extensions or buried drain lines</strong> — added cost, and the part that actually protects the foundation.</li>
      <li><strong>Gutter guards</strong> — roughly $8 to $15 per linear foot; fascia repair priced separately once the old gutter is off.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The cheapest bid usually skimps on the two things that matter most here: gutter size and where the water ends up. Both are invisible until a storm proves them.</p>

    <h2>When to Act</h2>

    <p>Late winter into spring, before the heavy storm season, is the smart window — you want the system moving water correctly before the first big Morgan County downpours, not after one has already found your crawlspace. If your gutters overflowed last spring, don't wait for a repeat; the water that sheets behind an overflowing gutter is what rots fascia and heaves the clay against the foundation. And if downspouts currently dump at the base of the house, extending them is one of the cheapest, highest-value fixes a homeowner can make.</p>

    <h2>Local Resources and the Storm Connection</h2>

    <p>Because gutters, roofs, and foundation drainage all move the same water, they're really one system, and the <a href="/locations/hartselle/">Hartselle contractor overview</a> maps how those exterior trades connect across a storm-prone Morgan County home. The installer who understands the clay-and-storm reality — not just how to hang aluminum — is the one worth calling.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Do gutters really matter that much here?</strong></p>
    <p>Yes — on expansive clay they're foundation protection. Roof water dumped at the footing swells the clay and cracks foundations. Where the downspouts discharge is the whole point.</p>

    <p><strong>Why do my gutters overflow in storms?</strong></p>
    <p>Usually undersized 5-inch gutters and 2x3 downspouts that can't handle Morgan County storm volume. Going to 6-inch with 3x4 downspouts fixes it.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>5-inch runs $7 to $10 a foot, 6-inch $10 to $14, plus downspout extensions that carry the water away from the foundation.</p>

